{"step_index":94,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-25T16:51:04Z","thinking":"Qt property keyword arguments are functional but lead to inelegant code, specifically with anonymous object instantiation. This approach, while technically sound, is less than ideal.\n\nThe user's central objective involves viewing an IPTV list flawlessly within SLOCAST, simultaneously streaming from this computer to a Xiaomi TV and their primary TV.\n\n\nThe IPTV provider likely limits concurrent connections, leading to stream interruptions when multiple devices directly access the same stream. Direct connections from both the PC and the Xiaomi TV to the IPTV source result in the provider detecting and blocking simultaneous streams. The relay server's implementation causes the PC *and* the Xiaomi TV to connect directly to the IPTV source, thereby triggering the provider's restriction.\n\n\nA single upstream connection from the PC to the IPTV provider is required, with the PC acting as a relay for other devices to avoid connection limits. The PC receives and buffers the stream, and then distributes it locally. External devices access the stream via the local relay server. Offering the full M3U playlist on the relay is also an option, enabling browsing from the TV's own IPTV applications.\n\n\nIndependent `urllib.request.urlopen` calls for each connected client create multiple TCP connections, violating the provider's connection limit. Implementing a `SingleStreamBroadcaster` is required, establishing a single connection to the IPTV stream, buffering the data, and relaying it to all connected devices. This design ensures that the IPTV provider sees only a single connection from the PC, regardless of how many devices are streaming. The Xiaomi TV can use apps like VLC or Nova Video Player to stream from the local relay.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"systemctl is-active firewalld 2>&1\nsudo -n firewall-cmd --list-ports 2>&1 || echo \"NO_SUDO_FIREWALL\"\nss -tulpn | grep 8765 2>&1","Cwd":"/home/slone","WaitMsBeforeAsync":5000,"toolAction":"Checking firewalld and port 8765","toolSummary":"Comprobar estado del cortafuegos y puerto 8765"}}]}
